#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "Submitting Patches"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Guidelines"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:9
msgid ""
"Here are some guidelines for contributing back to this project. There is "
"also a link:MyFirstContribution.html[step-by-step tutorial] available which "
"covers many of these same guidelines."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "A typical life cycle of a patch series"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:16
msgid ""
"To help us understand the reason behind various guidelines given later in "
"the document, first let's understand how the life cycle of a typical patch "
"series for this project goes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:19
msgid ""
"You come up with an itch.  You code it up.  You do not need any "
"pre-authorization from the project to do so."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:27
msgid ""
"Your patches will be reviewed by other contributors on the mailing list, and "
"the reviews will be done to assess the merit of various things, like the "
"general idea behind your patch (including \"is it solving a problem worth "
"solving in the first place?\"), the reason behind the design of the "
"solution, and the actual implementation.  The guidelines given here are "
"there to help your patches by making them easier to understand by the "
"reviewers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:33
msgid ""
"You send the patches to the list and cc people who may need to know about "
"the change.  Your goal is *not* necessarily to convince others that what you "
"are building is good.  Your goal is to get help in coming up with a solution "
"for the \"itch\" that is better than what you can build alone."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:40
msgid ""
"The people who may need to know are the ones who worked on the code you are "
"touching.  These people happen to be the ones who are most likely to be "
"knowledgeable enough to help you, but they have no obligation to help you "
"(i.e. you ask them for help, you don't demand).  +git log -p {litdd} "
"_$area_you_are_modifying_+ would help you find out who they are."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:45
msgid ""
"You get comments and suggestions for improvements.  You may even get them in "
"an \"on top of your change\" patch form.  You are expected to respond to "
"them with \"Reply-All\" on the mailing list, while taking them into account "
"while preparing an updated set of patches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:48
msgid ""
"Polish, refine, and re-send your patches to the list and to the people who "
"spent their time to improve your patch.  Go back to step (2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:55
msgid ""
"While the above iterations improve your patches, the maintainer may pick the "
"patches up from the list and queue them to the `seen` branch, in order to "
"make it easier for people to play with it without having to pick up and "
"apply the patches to their trees themselves.  Being in `seen` has no other "
"meaning.  Specifically, it does not mean the patch was \"accepted\" in any "
"way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:62
msgid ""
"When the discussion reaches a consensus that the latest iteration of the "
"patches are in good enough shape, the maintainer includes the topic in the "
"\"What's cooking\" report that are sent out a few times a week to the "
"mailing list, marked as \"Will merge to 'next'.\" This decision is primarily "
"made by the maintainer with help from those who participated in the review "
"discussion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:72
msgid ""
"After the patches are merged to the 'next' branch, the discussion can still "
"continue to further improve them by adding more patches on top, but by the "
"time a topic gets merged to 'next', it is expected that everybody agrees "
"that the scope and the basic direction of the topic are appropriate, so such "
"an incremental updates are limited to small corrections and polishing.  "
"After a topic cooks for some time (like 7 calendar days) in 'next' without "
"needing further tweaks on top, it gets merged to the 'master' branch and "
"wait to become part of the next major release."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:75
msgid ""
"In the following sections, many techniques and conventions are listed to "
"help your patches get reviewed effectively in such a life cycle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:78
#, no-wrap
msgid "Choose a starting point."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:84
msgid ""
"As a preliminary step, you must first choose a starting point for your "
"work. Typically this means choosing a branch, although technically speaking "
"it is actually a particular commit (typically the HEAD, or tip, of the "
"branch)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:87
msgid ""
"There are several important branches to be aware of. Namely, there are four "
"integration branches as discussed in linkgit:gitworkflows[7]:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:89
msgid "maint"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:90
msgid "master"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:91
msgid "next"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:92
msgid "seen"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:97
msgid ""
"The branches lower on the list are typically descendants of the ones that "
"come before it. For example, `maint` is an \"older\" branch than `master` "
"because `master` usually has patches (commits) on top of `maint`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:105
msgid ""
"There are also \"topic\" branches, which contain work from other "
"contributors.  Topic branches are created by the Git maintainer (in their "
"fork) to organize the current set of incoming contributions on the mailing "
"list, and are itemized in the regular \"What's cooking in git.git\" "
"announcements.  To find the tip of a topic branch, run `git log "
"--first-parent master..seen` and look for the merge commit. The second "
"parent of this commit is the tip of the topic branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:112
msgid ""
"There is one guiding principle for choosing the right starting point: in "
"general, always base your work on the oldest integration branch that your "
"change is relevant to (see \"Merge upwards\" in linkgit:gitworkflows[7]).  "
"What this principle means is that for the vast majority of cases, the "
"starting point for new work should be the latest HEAD commit of `maint` or "
"`master` based on the following cases:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:117
msgid ""
"If you are fixing bugs in the released version, use `maint` as the starting "
"point (which may mean you have to fix things without using new API features "
"on the cutting edge that recently appeared in `master` but were not "
"available in the released version)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:119
msgid "Otherwise (such as if you are adding new features) use `master`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:131
msgid ""
"In exceptional cases, a bug that was introduced in an old version may have "
"to be fixed for users of releases that are much older than the recent "
"releases.  `git describe --contains X` may describe `X` as "
"`v2.30.0-rc2-gXXXXXX` for the commit `X` that introduced the bug, and the "
"bug may be so high-impact that we may need to issue a new maintenance "
"release for Git 2.30.x series, when \"Git 2.41.0\" is the current release.  "
"In such a case, you may want to use the tip of the maintenance branch for "
"the 2.30.x series, which may be available in the `maint-2.30` branch in "
"https://github.com/gitster/git[the maintainer's \"broken out\" repo]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:142
msgid ""
"This also means that `next` or `seen` are inappropriate starting points for "
"your work, if you want your work to have a realistic chance of graduating to "
"`master`.  They are simply not designed to be used as a base for new work; "
"they are only there to make sure that topics in flight work well "
"together. This is why both `next` and `seen` are frequently re-integrated "
"with incoming patches on the mailing list and force-pushed to replace "
"previous versions of themselves. A topic that is literally built on top of "
"`next` cannot be merged to `master` without dragging in all the other topics "
"in `next`, some of which may not be ready."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:153
msgid ""
"For example, if you are making tree-wide changes, while somebody else is "
"also making their own tree-wide changes, your work may have severe overlap "
"with the other person's work.  This situation may tempt you to use `next` as "
"your starting point (because it would have the other person's work included "
"in it), but doing so would mean you'll not only depend on the other person's "
"work, but all the other random things from other contributors that are "
"already integrated into `next`.  And as soon as `next` is updated with a new "
"version, all of your work will need to be rebased anyway in order for them "
"to be cleanly applied by the maintainer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:164
msgid ""
"Under truly exceptional circumstances where you absolutely must depend on a "
"select few topic branches that are already in `next` but not in `master`, "
"you may want to create your own custom base-branch by forking `master` and "
"merging the required topic branches into it. You could then work on top of "
"this base-branch.  But keep in mind that this base-branch would only be "
"known privately to you.  So when you are ready to send your patches to the "
"list, be sure to communicate how you created it in your cover letter.  This "
"critical piece of information would allow others to recreate your "
"base-branch on their end in order for them to try out your work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:168
msgid ""
"Finally, note that some parts of the system have dedicated maintainers with "
"their own separate source code repositories (see the section \"Subsystems\" "
"below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:170
#, no-wrap
msgid "Make separate commits for logically separate changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:177
msgid ""
"Unless your patch is really trivial, you should not be sending out a patch "
"that was generated between your working tree and your commit head.  Instead, "
"always make a commit with complete commit message and generate a series of "
"patches from your repository.  It is a good discipline."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:182
msgid ""
"Give an explanation for the change(s) that is detailed enough so that people "
"can judge if it is good thing to do, without reading the actual patch text "
"to determine how well the code does what the explanation promises to do."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:192
msgid ""
"If your description starts to get too long, that's a sign that you probably "
"need to split up your commit to finer grained pieces.  That being said, "
"patches which plainly describe the things that help reviewers check the "
"patch, and future maintainers understand the code, are the most beautiful "
"patches.  Descriptions that summarize the point in the subject well, and "
"describe the motivation for the change, the approach taken by the change, "
"and if relevant how this differs substantially from the prior version, are "
"all good things to have."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:195
msgid ""
"Make sure that you have tests for the bug you are fixing.  See `t/README` "
"for guidance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:206
msgid ""
"When adding a new feature, make sure that you have new tests to show the "
"feature triggers the new behavior when it should, and to show the feature "
"does not trigger when it shouldn't.  After any code change, make sure that "
"the entire test suite passes.  When fixing a bug, make sure you have new "
"tests that break if somebody else breaks what you fixed by accident to avoid "
"regression.  Also, try merging your work to 'next' and 'seen' and make sure "
"the tests still pass; topics by others that are still in flight may have "
"unexpected interactions with what you are trying to do in your topic."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:210
msgid ""
"Pushing to a fork of https://github.com/git/git will use their CI "
"integration to test your changes on Linux, Mac and Windows. See the "
"<<GHCI,GitHub CI>> section for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:214
msgid ""
"Do not forget to update the documentation to describe the updated behavior "
"and make sure that the resulting documentation set formats well (try the "
"Documentation/doc-diff script)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:226
msgid ""
"We currently have a liberal mixture of US and UK English norms for spelling "
"and grammar, which is somewhat unfortunate.  A huge patch that touches the "
"files all over the place only to correct the inconsistency is not welcome, "
"though.  Potential clashes with other changes that can result from such a "
"patch are not worth it.  We prefer to gradually reconcile the "
"inconsistencies in favor of US English, with small and easily digestible "
"patches, as a side effect of doing some other real work in the vicinity "
"(e.g. rewriting a paragraph for clarity, while turning en_UK spelling to "
"en_US).  Obvious typographical fixes are much more welcomed (\"teh -> "
"\"the\"), preferably submitted as independent patches separate from other "
"documentation changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:232
msgid ""
"Oh, another thing.  We are picky about whitespaces.  Make sure your changes "
"do not trigger errors with the sample pre-commit hook shipped in "
"`templates/hooks--pre-commit`.  To help ensure this does not happen, run "
"`git diff --check` on your changes before you commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:234
#, no-wrap
msgid "Describe your changes well."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:242
msgid ""
"The log message that explains your changes is just as important as the "
"changes themselves.  Your code may be clearly written with in-code comment "
"to sufficiently explain how it works with the surrounding code, but those "
"who need to fix or enhance your code in the future will need to know _why_ "
"your code does what it does, for a few reasons:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:248
msgid ""
"Your code may be doing something differently from what you wanted it to do.  "
"Writing down what you actually wanted to achieve will help them fix your "
"code and make it do what it should have been doing (also, you often discover "
"your own bugs yourself, while writing the log message to summarize the "
"thought behind it)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:261
msgid ""
"Your code may be doing things that were only necessary for your immediate "
"needs (e.g. \"do X to directories\" without implementing or even designing "
"what is to be done on files).  Writing down why you excluded what the code "
"does not do will help guide future developers.  Writing down \"we do X to "
"directories, because directories have characteristic Y\" would help them "
"infer \"oh, files also have the same characteristic Y, so perhaps doing X to "
"them would also make sense?\".  Saying \"we don't do the same X to files, "
"because ...\" will help them decide if the reasoning is sound (in which case "
"they do not waste time extending your code to cover files), or reason "
"differently (in which case, they can explain why they extend your code to "
"cover files, too)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:265
msgid ""
"The goal of your log message is to convey the _why_ behind your change to "
"help future developers.  The reviewers will also make sure that your "
"proposed log message will serve this purpose well."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:271
msgid ""
"The first line of the commit message should be a short description (50 "
"characters is the soft limit, see DISCUSSION in linkgit:git-commit[1]), and "
"should skip the full stop.  It is also conventional in most cases to prefix "
"the first line with \"area: \" where the area is a filename or identifier "
"for the general area of the code being modified, e.g."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:273
msgid "doc: clarify distinction between sign-off and pgp-signing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:274
msgid "githooks.txt: improve the intro section"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:277
msgid ""
"If in doubt which identifier to use, run `git log --no-merges` on the files "
"you are modifying to see the current conventions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:288
msgid ""
"The title sentence after the \"area:\" prefix omits the full stop at the "
"end, and its first word is not capitalized (the omission of capitalization "
"applies only to the word after the \"area:\" prefix of the title) unless "
"there is a reason to capitalize it other than because it is the first word "
"in the sentence.  E.g. \"doc: clarify...\", not \"doc: Clarify...\", or "
"\"githooks.txt: improve...\", not \"githooks.txt: Improve...\".  But \"refs: "
"HEAD is also treated as a ref\" is correct, as we spell `HEAD` in all caps "
"even when it appears in the middle of a sentence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:291
msgid "The body should provide a meaningful commit message, which:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:294
msgid ""
"explains the problem the change tries to solve, i.e. what is wrong with the "
"current code without the change."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:297
msgid ""
"justifies the way the change solves the problem, i.e. why the result with "
"the change is better."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:299
msgid "alternate solutions considered but discarded, if any."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:306
msgid ""
"The problem statement that describes the status quo is written in the "
"present tense.  Write \"The code does X when it is given input Y\", instead "
"of \"The code used to do Y when given input X\".  You do not have to say "
"\"Currently\"---the status quo in the problem statement is about the code "
"_without_ your change, by project convention."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:314
msgid ""
"Describe your changes in imperative mood, e.g. \"make xyzzy do frotz\" "
"instead of \"[This patch] makes xyzzy do frotz\" or \"[I] changed xyzzy to "
"do frotz\", as if you are giving orders to the codebase to change its "
"behavior.  Try to make sure your explanation can be understood without "
"external resources. Instead of giving a URL to a mailing list archive, "
"summarize the relevant points of the discussion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:320
msgid ""
"There are a few reasons why you may want to refer to another commit in the "
"\"more stable\" part of the history (i.e. on branches like `maint`, "
"`master`, and `next`):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:322
msgid "A commit that introduced the root cause of a bug you are fixing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:324
msgid "A commit that introduced a feature that you are enhancing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:327
msgid ""
"A commit that conflicts with your work when you made a trial merge of your "
"work into `next` and `seen` for testing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:331
msgid ""
"When you reference a commit on a more stable branch (like `master`, `maint` "
"and `next`), use the format \"abbreviated hash (subject, date)\", like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:335
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tCommit f86a374 (pack-bitmap.c: fix a memleak, 2015-03-30)\n"
"\tnoticed that ...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:340
msgid ""
"The \"Copy commit reference\" command of gitk can be used to obtain this "
"format (with the subject enclosed in a pair of double-quotes), or this "
"invocation of `git show`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:343
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tgit show -s --pretty=reference <commit>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:346
msgid "or, on an older version of Git without support for --pretty=reference:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:349
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tgit show -s --date=short --pretty='format:%h (%s, %ad)' <commit>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:352
#, no-wrap
msgid "Certify your work by adding your `Signed-off-by` trailer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:358
msgid ""
"To improve tracking of who did what, we ask you to certify that you wrote "
"the patch or have the right to pass it on under the same license as ours, by "
"\"signing off\" your patch.  Without sign-off, we cannot accept your "
"patches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:360
msgid "If (and only if) you certify the below D-C-O:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:362
#, no-wrap
msgid "Developer's Certificate of Origin 1.1"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:365
msgid "By making a contribution to this project, I certify that:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:369
msgid ""
"The contribution was created in whole or in part by me and I have the right "
"to submit it under the open source license indicated in the file; or"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:377
msgid ""
"The contribution is based upon previous work that, to the best of my "
"knowledge, is covered under an appropriate open source license and I have "
"the right under that license to submit that work with modifications, whether "
"created in whole or in part by me, under the same open source license "
"(unless I am permitted to submit under a different license), as indicated in "
"the file; or"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:381
msgid ""
"The contribution was provided directly to me by some other person who "
"certified (a), (b) or (c) and I have not modified it."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:387
msgid ""
"I understand and agree that this project and the contribution are public and "
"that a record of the contribution (including all personal information I "
"submit with it, including my sign-off) is maintained indefinitely and may be "
"redistributed consistent with this project or the open source license(s) "
"involved."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:390
#, no-wrap
msgid "Developer's Certificate of Origin 1.1(JP)"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:393
msgid "(JP)By making a contribution to this project, I certify that:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:397
msgid ""
"(JP)The contribution was created in whole or in part by me and I have the "
"right to submit it under the open source license indicated in the file; or"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:405
msgid ""
"(JP)The contribution is based upon previous work that, to the best of my "
"knowledge, is covered under an appropriate open source license and I have "
"the right under that license to submit that work with modifications, whether "
"created in whole or in part by me, under the same open source license "
"(unless I am permitted to submit under a different license), as indicated in "
"the file; or"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:409
msgid ""
"(JP)The contribution was provided directly to me by some other person who "
"certified (a), (b) or (c) and I have not modified it."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block _
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:415
msgid ""
"(JP)I understand and agree that this project and the contribution are public "
"and that a record of the contribution (including all personal information I "
"submit with it, including my sign-off) is maintained indefinitely and may be "
"redistributed consistent with this project or the open source license(s) "
"involved."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:419
msgid "you add a \"Signed-off-by\" trailer to your commit, that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:422
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tSigned-off-by: Random J Developer <random@developer.example.org>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:426
msgid ""
"This line can be added by Git if you run the git-commit command with the -s "
"option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:432
msgid ""
"Notice that you can place your own `Signed-off-by` trailer when forwarding "
"somebody else's patch with the above rules for D-C-O.  Indeed you are "
"encouraged to do so.  Do not forget to place an in-body \"From: \" line at "
"the beginning to properly attribute the change to its true author (see (2) "
"above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:437
msgid ""
"This procedure originally came from the Linux kernel project, so our rule is "
"quite similar to theirs, but what exactly it means to sign-off your patch "
"differs from project to project, so it may be different from that of the "
"project you are accustomed to."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:445
msgid ""
"Please use a known identity in the `Signed-off-by` trailer, since we cannot "
"accept anonymous contributions. It is common, but not required, to use some "
"form of your real name. We realize that some contributors are not "
"comfortable doing so or prefer to contribute under a pseudonym or preferred "
"name and we can accept your patch either way, as long as the name and email "
"you use are distinctive, identifying, and not misleading."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:448
msgid ""
"The goal of this policy is to allow us to have sufficient information to "
"contact you if questions arise about your contribution."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:451
msgid "If you like, you can put extra trailers at the end:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:454
msgid ""
"`Reported-by:` is used to credit someone who found the bug that the patch "
"attempts to fix."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:456
msgid ""
"`Acked-by:` says that the person who is more familiar with the area the "
"patch attempts to modify liked the patch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:459
msgid ""
"`Reviewed-by:`, unlike the other trailers, can only be offered by the "
"reviewers themselves when they are completely satisfied with the patch after "
"a detailed analysis."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:461
msgid ""
"`Tested-by:` is used to indicate that the person applied the patch and found "
"it to have the desired effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:463
msgid ""
"`Co-authored-by:` is used to indicate that people exchanged drafts of a "
"patch before submitting it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:465
msgid ""
"`Helped-by:` is used to credit someone who suggested ideas for changes "
"without providing the precise changes in patch form."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:467
msgid ""
"`Mentored-by:` is used to credit someone with helping develop a patch as "
"part of a mentorship program (e.g., GSoC or Outreachy)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:469
msgid ""
"`Suggested-by:` is used to credit someone with suggesting the idea for a "
"patch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:473
msgid ""
"While you can also create your own trailer if the situation warrants it, we "
"encourage you to instead use one of the common trailers in this project "
"highlighted above."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:476
msgid ""
"Only capitalize the very first letter of the trailer, i.e. favor "
"\"Signed-off-by\" over \"Signed-Off-By\" and \"Acked-by:\" over "
"\"Acked-By\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:478
#, no-wrap
msgid "Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:485
msgid ""
"The Developer's Certificate of Origin requires contributors to certify that "
"they know the origin of their contributions to the project and that they "
"have the right to submit it under the project's license.  It's not yet clear "
"that this can be legally satisfied when submitting significant amount of "
"content that has been generated by AI tools."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:489
msgid ""
"Another issue with AI generated content is that AIs still often hallucinate "
"or just produce bad code, commit messages, documentation or output, even "
"when you point out their mistakes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:494
msgid ""
"To avoid these issues, we will reject anything that looks AI generated, that "
"sounds overly formal or bloated, that looks like AI slop, that looks good on "
"the surface but makes no sense, or that senders don’t understand or cannot "
"explain."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:496
msgid "We strongly recommend using AI tools carefully and responsibly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:504
msgid ""
"Contributors would often benefit more from AI by using it to guide and help "
"them step by step towards producing a solution by themselves rather than by "
"asking for a full solution that they would then mostly copy-paste. They can "
"also use AI to help with debugging, or with checking for obvious mistakes, "
"things that can be improved, things that don’t match our style, guidelines "
"or our feedback, before sending it to us."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:506
#, no-wrap
msgid "Generate your patch using Git tools out of your commits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:509
msgid "Git based diff tools generate unidiff which is the preferred format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:513
msgid ""
"You do not have to be afraid to use `-M` option to `git diff` or `git "
"format-patch`, if your patch involves file renames.  The receiving end can "
"handle them just fine."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:521
msgid ""
"Please make sure your patch does not add commented out debugging code, or "
"include any extra files which do not relate to what your patch is trying to "
"achieve. Make sure to review your patch after generating it, to ensure "
"accuracy.  Before sending out, please make sure it cleanly applies to the "
"starting point you have chosen in the \"Choose a starting point\" section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:526
msgid ""
"From the perspective of those reviewing your patch, the `master` branch is "
"the default expected starting point.  So if you have chosen a different "
"starting point, please communicate this choice in your cover letter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:529
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sending your patches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ====
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:531
#, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing your reviewers"
msgstr ""

#. :security-ml: footnoteref:[security-ml,The Git Security mailing list: git-security@googlegroups.com]
#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:540
msgid ""
"Patches that may be security relevant should be submitted privately to the "
"Git Security mailing list footnote:[The Git Security mailing list: "
"git-security@googlegroups.com], instead of the public mailing list."
msgstr ""

#. :contrib-scripts: footnoteref:[contrib-scripts,Scripts under `contrib/` are not part of the core `git` binary and must be called directly. Clone the Git codebase and run `perl contrib/contacts/git-contacts`.]
#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:554
msgid ""
"Send your patch with \"To:\" set to the mailing list, with \"cc:\" listing "
"people who are involved in the area you are touching (the `git-contacts` "
"script in `contrib/contacts/` footnote:[Scripts under `contrib/` are not "
"part of the core `git` binary and must be called directly. Clone the Git "
"codebase and run `perl contrib/contacts/git-contacts`.] can help to identify "
"them), to solicit comments and reviews.  Also, when you made trial merges of "
"your topic to `next` and `seen`, you may have noticed work by others "
"conflicting with your changes.  There is a good possibility that these "
"people may know the area you are touching well."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:557
msgid ""
"If you are using `send-email`, you can feed it the output of `git-contacts` "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:560
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tgit send-email --cc-cmd='perl contrib/contacts/git-contacts' "
"feature/*.patch\n"
msgstr ""

#. :current-maintainer: footnote:[The current maintainer: gitster@pobox.com]
#. :git-ml: footnote:[The mailing list: git@vger.kernel.org]
#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:572
msgid ""
"After the list reached a consensus that it is a good idea to apply the "
"patch, re-send it with \"To:\" set to the maintainer footnote:[The current "
"maintainer: gitster@pobox.com] and \"cc:\" the list footnote:[The mailing "
"list: git@vger.kernel.org] for inclusion.  This is especially relevant when "
"the maintainer did not heavily participate in the discussion and instead "
"left the review to trusted others."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:576
msgid ""
"Do not forget to add trailers such as `Acked-by:`, `Reviewed-by:` and "
"`Tested-by:` lines as necessary to credit people who helped your patch, and "
"\"cc:\" them when sending such a final version for inclusion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ====
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:577
#, no-wrap
msgid "`format-patch` and `send-email`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:583
msgid ""
"Learn to use `format-patch` and `send-email` if possible.  These commands "
"are optimized for the workflow of sending patches, avoiding many ways your "
"existing e-mail client (often optimized for \"multipart/*\" MIME type "
"e-mails) might render your patches unusable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:587
msgid ""
"Here we outline the procedure using `format-patch` and `send-email`, but you "
"can instead use GitGitGadget to send in your patches (see "
"link:MyFirstContribution.html[MyFirstContribution])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:594
msgid ""
"People on the Git mailing list need to be able to read and comment on the "
"changes you are submitting.  It is important for a developer to be able to "
"\"quote\" your changes, using standard e-mail tools, so that they may "
"comment on specific portions of your code.  For this reason, each patch "
"should be submitted \"inline\" in a separate message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:601
msgid ""
"All subsequent versions of a patch series and other related patches should "
"be grouped into their own e-mail thread to help readers find all parts of "
"the series.  To that end, send them as replies to either an additional "
"\"cover letter\" message (see below), the first patch, or the respective "
"preceding patch.  Here is a "
"link:MyFirstContribution.html#v2-git-send-email[step-by-step guide] on how "
"to submit updated versions of a patch series."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:605
msgid ""
"If your log message (including your name on the `Signed-off-by` trailer) is "
"not writable in ASCII, make sure that you send off a message in the correct "
"encoding."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:609
msgid ""
"Be wary of your MUAs word-wrap corrupting your patch.  Do not cut-n-paste "
"your patch; you can lose tabs that way if you are not careful."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:619
msgid ""
"It is a common convention to prefix your subject line with [PATCH].  This "
"lets people easily distinguish patches from other e-mail discussions.  Use "
"of markers in addition to PATCH within the brackets to describe the nature "
"of the patch is also encouraged.  E.g. [RFC PATCH] (where RFC stands for "
"\"request for comments\") is often used to indicate a patch needs further "
"discussion before being accepted, [PATCH v2], [PATCH v3] etc.  are often "
"seen when you are sending an update to what you have previously sent."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:632
msgid ""
"The `git format-patch` command follows the best current practice to format "
"the body of an e-mail message.  At the beginning of the patch should come "
"your commit message, ending with the `Signed-off-by` trailers, and a line "
"that consists of three dashes, followed by the diffstat information and the "
"patch itself.  If you are forwarding a patch from somebody else, optionally, "
"at the beginning of the e-mail message just before the commit message "
"starts, you can put a \"From: \" line to name that person.  To change the "
"default \"[PATCH]\" in the subject to \"[<text>]\", use `git format-patch "
"--subject-prefix=<text>`.  As a shortcut, you can use `--rfc` instead of "
"`--subject-prefix=\"RFC PATCH\"`, or `-v <n>` instead of "
"`--subject-prefix=\"PATCH v<n>\"`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:640
msgid ""
"You often want to add additional explanation about the patch, other than the "
"commit message itself.  Place such \"cover letter\" material between the "
"three-dash line and the diffstat.  For patches requiring multiple iterations "
"of review and discussion, an explanation of changes between each iteration "
"can be kept in Git-notes and inserted automatically following the three-dash "
"line via `git format-patch --notes`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:643
#, no-wrap
msgid "*This is EXPERIMENTAL*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:657
msgid ""
"When sending a topic, you can optionally propose a topic name and/or a "
"one-paragraph summary that should appear in the \"What's cooking\" report "
"when it is picked up to explain the topic.  If you choose to do so, please "
"write a 2-5 line paragraph that will fit well in our release notes (see many "
"bulleted entries in the Documentation/RelNotes/* files for examples), and "
"make it the first (or second, if including a suggested topic name) paragraph "
"of the cover letter.  If suggesting a topic name, use the format "
"\"XX/your-topic-name\", where \"XX\" is a stand-in for the primary author's "
"initials, and \"your-topic-name\" is a brief, dash-delimited description of "
"what your topic does.  For a single-patch series, use the space between the "
"three-dash line and the diffstat, as described earlier."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:665
msgid ""
"If your patch series is part of a larger effort spanning multiple patch "
"series, briefly describe the broader goal, and state where the current "
"series fits into that goal.  If you are suggesting a topic name as in "
"<<the-topic-summary, section above>>, consider "
"\"XX/the-broader-goal-part-one\", \"XX/the-broader-goal-part-two\", and so "
"on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:677
msgid ""
"Do not attach the patch as a MIME attachment, compressed or not.  Do not let "
"your e-mail client send quoted-printable.  Do not let your e-mail client "
"send format=flowed which would destroy whitespaces in your patches. Many "
"popular e-mail applications will not always transmit a MIME attachment as "
"plain text, making it impossible to comment on your code.  A MIME attachment "
"also takes a bit more time to process.  This does not decrease the "
"likelihood of your MIME-attached change being accepted, but it makes it more "
"likely that it will be postponed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:680
msgid ""
"Exception: If your mailer is mangling patches then someone may ask you to "
"re-send them using MIME, that is OK."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:687
msgid ""
"Do not PGP sign your patch. Most likely, your maintainer or other people on "
"the list would not have your PGP key and would not bother obtaining it "
"anyway.  Your patch is not judged by who you are; a good patch from an "
"unknown origin has a far better chance of being accepted than a patch from a "
"known, respected origin that is done poorly or does incorrect things."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:692
msgid ""
"If you really really really really want to do a PGP signed patch, format it "
"as \"multipart/signed\", not a text/plain message that starts with "
"`-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----`.  That is not a text/plain, it's "
"something else."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:693
#, no-wrap
msgid "Handling Conflicts and Iterating Patches"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:699
msgid ""
"When revising changes made to your patches, it's important to acknowledge "
"the possibility of conflicts with other ongoing topics. To navigate these "
"potential conflicts effectively, follow the recommended steps outlined "
"below:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:703
msgid ""
"Build on a suitable base branch, see the <<choose-starting-point, section "
"above>>, and format-patch the series. If you are doing \"rebase -i\" "
"in-place to update from the previous round, this will reuse the previous "
"base so (2) and (3) may become trivial."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:705
msgid "Find the base of where the last round was queued"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:708
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ mine='kn/ref-transaction-symref'\n"
"$ git checkout \"origin/seen^{/^Merge branch '$mine'}...master\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:710
msgid "Apply your format-patch result.  There are two cases"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:711
msgid "Things apply cleanly and tests fine.  Go to (4)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:713
msgid ""
"Things apply cleanly but does not build or test fails, or things do not "
"apply cleanly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:718
msgid ""
"In the latter case, you have textual or semantic conflicts coming from the "
"difference between the old base and the base you used to build in (1).  "
"Identify what caused the breakages (e.g., a topic or two may have merged "
"since the base used by (2) until the base used by (1))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:724
msgid ""
"Check out the latest 'origin/master' (which may be newer than the base used "
"by (2)), \"merge --no-ff\" the topics you newly depend on in there, and use "
"the result of the merge(s) as the base, rebuild the series and test again.  "
"Run format-patch from the last such merges to the tip of your topic.  If you "
"did"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:728
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    $ git checkout origin/master\n"
"    $ git merge --no-ff --into-name kn/ref-transaction-symref fo/obar\n"
"    $ git merge --no-ff --into-name kn/ref-transaction-symref ba/zqux\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:729
#, no-wrap
msgid "rebuild the topic ...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:732
msgid ""
"Then you'd just format your topic above these \"preparing the ground\" "
"merges, e.g."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:734
#, no-wrap
msgid "    $ git format-patch \"HEAD^{/^Merge branch 'ba/zqux'}\"..HEAD\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:737
msgid ""
"Do not forget to write in the cover letter you did this, including the "
"topics you have in your base on top of 'master'.  Then go to (4)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:739
msgid "Make a trial merge of your topic into 'next' and 'seen', e.g."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:743
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git checkout --detach 'origin/seen'\n"
"$ git revert -m 1 <the merge of the previous iteration into seen>\n"
"$ git merge kn/ref-transaction-symref\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:749
msgid ""
"The \"revert\" is needed if the previous iteration of your topic is already "
"in 'seen' (like in this case).  You could choose to rebuild "
"master..origin/seen from scratch while excluding your previous iteration, "
"which may emulate what happens on the maintainers end more closely."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:755
msgid ""
"This trial merge may conflict.  It is primarily to see what conflicts "
"_other_ topics may have with your topic.  In other words, you do not have to "
"depend on it to make your topic work on 'master'.  It may become the job of "
"the other topic owners to resolve conflicts if your topic goes to 'next' "
"before theirs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:759
msgid ""
"Make a note on what conflict you saw in the cover letter.  You do not "
"necessarily have to resolve them, but it would be a good opportunity to "
"learn what others are doing in related areas."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:762
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    $ git checkout --detach 'origin/next'\n"
"    $ git merge kn/ref-transaction-symref\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:770
msgid ""
"This is to see what conflicts your topic has with other topics that are "
"already cooking.  This should not conflict if (3)-2 prepared a base on top "
"of updated master plus dependent topics taken from 'next'.  Unless the "
"context is severe (one way to tell is try the same trial merge with your old "
"iteration, which may conflict in a similar way), expect that it will be "
"handled on maintainers end (if it gets unmanageable, I'll ask to rebase when "
"I receive your patches)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:771
#, no-wrap
msgid "Subsystems with dedicated maintainers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:775
msgid ""
"Some parts of the system have dedicated maintainers with their own "
"repositories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:777
msgid "`git-gui/` comes from the git-gui project, maintained by Johannes Sixt:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:779
#, no-wrap
msgid "https://github.com/j6t/git-gui\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:781
msgid "Contibutions should go via the git mailing list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:783
msgid "`gitk-git/` comes from the gitk project, maintained by Johannes Sixt:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:785
#, no-wrap
msgid "https://github.com/j6t/gitk\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:787
#, no-wrap
msgid "Contibutions should go via the git mailing list.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:789
msgid "`po/` comes from the localization coordinator, Jiang Xin:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:791
#, no-wrap
msgid "https://github.com/git-l10n/git-po/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:793
msgid "Patches to these parts should be based on their trees."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:797
msgid ""
"The \"Git documentation translations\" project, led by Jean-Noël Avila, "
"translates our documentation pages.  Their work products are maintained "
"separately from this project, not as part of our tree:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:799
#, no-wrap
msgid "https://github.com/jnavila/git-manpages-l10n/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:801
#, no-wrap
msgid "GitHub CI[[GHCI]]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:807
msgid ""
"With an account at GitHub, you can use GitHub CI to test your changes on "
"Linux, Mac and Windows. See "
"https://github.com/git/git/actions/workflows/main.yml for examples of recent "
"CI runs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:809
msgid "Follow these steps for the initial setup:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:813
msgid ""
"Fork https://github.com/git/git to your GitHub account.  You can find "
"detailed instructions how to fork here: "
"https://help.github.com/articles/fork-a-repo/"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:817
msgid ""
"After the initial setup, CI will run whenever you push new changes to your "
"fork of Git on GitHub.  You can monitor the test state of all your branches "
"here: `https://github.com/<Your GitHub "
"handle>/git/actions/workflows/main.yml`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:824
msgid ""
"If a branch does not pass all test cases then it will be marked with a red "
"+x+, instead of a green check. In that case, you can click on the failing "
"job and navigate to \"ci/run-build-and-tests.sh\" and/or "
"\"ci/print-test-failures.sh\". You can also download \"Artifacts\" which are "
"zip archives containing tarred (or zipped) archives with test data relevant "
"for debugging."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:827
msgid ""
"Then fix the problem and push your fix to your GitHub fork. This will "
"trigger a new CI build to ensure all tests pass."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:829
#, no-wrap
msgid "MUA specific hints"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:834
msgid ""
"Some of the patches I receive or pick up from the list share common patterns "
"of breakage.  Please make sure your MUA is set up properly not to corrupt "
"whitespaces."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:838
msgid ""
"See the DISCUSSION section of linkgit:git-format-patch[1] for hints on "
"checking your patch by mailing it to yourself and applying with "
"linkgit:git-am[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:847
msgid ""
"While you are at it, check the resulting commit log message from a trial run "
"of applying the patch.  If what is in the resulting commit is not exactly "
"what you would want to see, it is very likely that your maintainer would end "
"up hand editing the log message when he applies your patch.  Things like "
"\"Hi, this is my first patch.\\n\", if you really want to put in the patch "
"e-mail, should come after the three-dash line that signals the end of the "
"commit message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:849
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pine"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:852
msgid "(Johannes Schindelin)"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:857
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"I don't know how many people still use pine, but for those poor\n"
"souls it may be good to mention that the quell-flowed-text is\n"
"needed for recent versions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:860
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"... the \"no-strip-whitespace-before-send\" option, too. AFAIK it\n"
"was introduced in 4.60.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:863
msgid "(Linus Torvalds)"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:866
#, no-wrap
msgid "And 4.58 needs at least this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:870
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"diff-tree 8326dd8350be64ac7fc805f6563a1d61ad10d32c (from "
"e886a61f76edf5410573e92e38ce22974f9c40f1)\n"
"Author: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@g5.osdl.org>\n"
"Date:   Mon Aug 15 17:23:51 2005 -0700\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:872
#, no-wrap
msgid "    Fix pine whitespace-corruption bug\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:875
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    There's no excuse for unconditionally removing whitespace from\n"
"    the pico buffers on close.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:888
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"diff --git a/pico/pico.c b/pico/pico.c\n"
"--- a/pico/pico.c\n"
"+++ b/pico/pico.c\n"
"@@ -219,7 +219,9 @@ PICO *pm;\n"
"\t    switch(pico_all_done){\t/* prepare for/handle final events */\n"
"\t      case COMP_EXIT :\t\t/* already confirmed */\n"
"\t\tpackheader();\n"
"+#if 0\n"
"\t\tstripwhitespace();\n"
"+#endif\n"
"\t\tc |= COMP_EXIT;\n"
"\t\tbreak;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:891
msgid "(Daniel Barkalow)"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:895
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> A patch to SubmittingPatches, MUA specific help section for\n"
"> users of Pine 4.63 would be very much appreciated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:902
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Ah, it looks like a recent version changed the default behavior to do the\n"
"right thing, and inverted the sense of the configuration option. (Either\n"
"that or Gentoo did it.) So you need to set the\n"
"\"no-strip-whitespace-before-send\" option, unless the option you have is\n"
"\"strip-whitespace-before-send\", in which case you should avoid checking\n"
"it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:904
#, no-wrap
msgid "Thunderbird, KMail, GMail"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:907
msgid "See the MUA-SPECIFIC HINTS section of linkgit:git-format-patch[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:908
#, no-wrap
msgid "Gnus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/SubmittingPatches.adoc:919
msgid ""
"\"|\" in the `*Summary*` buffer can be used to pipe the current message to "
"an external program, and this is a handy way to drive `git am`.  However, if "
"the message is MIME encoded, what is piped into the program is the "
"representation you see in your `*Article*` buffer after unwrapping MIME.  "
"This is often not what you would want for two reasons.  It tends to screw up "
"non-ASCII characters (most notably in people's names), and also whitespaces "
"(fatal in patches).  Running \"C-u g\" to display the message in raw form "
"before using \"|\" to run the pipe can work this problem around."
msgstr ""
